# TLS reverse proxy in front of the controller, for the bare-metal
# systemd path. uvicorn binds 127.0.0.1:8000 only (see
# kite-controller.service), so Caddy is the only thing listening on
# the network.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEFAULT: self-signed certificates, no DNS or public address needed.
#
# `local_certs` makes Caddy's own local CA the issuer for every site
# here, and `on_demand` mints a certificate for whatever name the client
# actually asks for — https://localhost/, https://192.168.1.50/,
# https://kite.lab/ all work off the same config with nothing to
# configure. That matters for a lab controller, which people reach by IP
# as often as by name.
#
# Both directives are required. `tls internal` alone, on a site address
# with no hostname, leaves the issuer with no subject name to put in a
# certificate: every TLS handshake then fails with "tlsv1 alert internal
# error" before any HTTP is exchanged, which makes the site unreachable
# rather than merely untrusted.
#
# Browsers do warn on first visit, because the CA issuing these
# certificates is local and not one they trust. That part is expected.
# Agents pointing at this controller's /repo path for
# `dnf install kite-agent` need `sslverify=0` to match — see
# controller/README.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
{
	local_certs
}

:443 {
	tls internal {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# REAL DOMAIN instead: if you have DNS pointing at this host, replace
# EVERYTHING above with the two lines below and Caddy handles Let's
# Encrypt automatically. Open port 80 too (used once for the ACME
# HTTP-01 challenge — controller-install.sh has the firewall line
# commented out for exactly this), then Caddy redirects 80 -> 443
# itself.
#
# Delete the `local_certs` block when you do — leaving it in forces the
# local CA and you'll get a self-signed certificate for your real
# domain, which is not what you want and looks like Let's Encrypt
# failing.
#
# kite.example.com {
# 	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
# }
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
